<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  The weather app's background check (IosWeatherRefresh in the shared module): a background
	  app refresh now and then, so "rain in 20 minutes" can arrive while the app is closed. The
	  identifier must be listed here or registering its handler at launch is a crash.
	-->
	<key>BGTaskSchedulerPermittedIdentifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>com.meticulouscreations.homesafe.weather.refresh</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>fetch</string>
	</array>
	<key>NSFaceIDUsageDescription</key>
	<string>HomeSafe uses Face ID to securely sign you in without retyping your password.</string>
	<!--
	  Automatic presence (docs/away-mode.md): a geofence around home flips this phone's away
	  switch. "While Using" is enough to set where home is; "Always" is what lets the fence
	  notice you leaving with the app closed. Weather (docs/weather.md) forecasts for where the
	  phone is: it keeps the last position on the phone and asks the forecast services about it,
	  rounded to about a kilometre. Both strings say so, since iOS shows them when it asks.
	-->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>HomeSafe uses your location to set where home is, so away mode can switch on by itself when everyone has left, and to show the weather where you are. For weather, your last position is kept on this phone and sent, rounded to about a kilometre, to the forecast services (Open-Meteo, and the US National Weather Service).</string>
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>With "Always", HomeSafe can notice you leaving or arriving home while the app is closed and switch away mode for you. For away mode only the home geofence is watched. For weather, your last position is kept on this phone and sent, rounded to about a kilometre, to the forecast services (Open-Meteo, and the US National Weather Service).</string>
	<!--
	  Frigate's go2rtc live-stream endpoint (port 1984) is plain HTTP by design — it relies
	  on the network boundary (Tailscale, or the home LAN when connected over the server's
	  private address) for trust rather than TLS. The authenticated Frigate API itself
	  (port 8971) is HTTPS and unaffected by this. Mirrors the Android
	  network_security_config.xml cleartext allowance for the same reason.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>